Paper 6
Deep learning workload prediction cloud
https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/8301555
Q. Zhang, L. T. Yang, Z. Yan, Z. Chen and P. Li, "An Efficient Deep Learning Model to Predict Cloud Workload for Industry Informatics," in IEEE Transactions on Industrial Informatics, vol. 14, no. 7, pp. 3170-3178, July 2018, doi: 10.1109/TII.2018.2808910.
Methods
CP-SAE (Canonical Polyadic - Stacked AutoEncoder) — a lighter, faster version of a deep learning model used to predict cloud workload.
The problem it solves: Normal deep learning models (like stacked autoencoders) have too many parameters, so training them takes a long time.
The trick used: A math technique called Canonical Polyadic (CP) decomposition is used to compress the model's weight matrices into smaller pieces (like breaking a big number into simpler building blocks), so there's much less to train.
How it works step by step:
Take a normal stacked autoencoder (a deep learning model that learns patterns by compressing then reconstructing data)
Convert its weight matrices into tensors (multi-dimensional arrays, like a cube of numbers instead of just a flat table)
Apply CP decomposition to break these tensors into smaller, simpler pieces (a sum of simple "rank-1" building blocks)
Train this compressed version using a modified backpropagation algorithm that works directly on the compressed pieces so training is much faster
End goal: Use this efficient model to predict CPU usage (workload) of virtual machines in the cloud, so cloud companies know in advance how much computing power to allocate (avoids wasting resources or running out of capacity)


Datasets
Source: PlanetLab — a real-world open cloud testing platform with 1000+ virtual machines across ~700 sites worldwide, recording CPU usage every 5 minutes
First 8 days of data = training, last 2 days = testing
STL-10 dataset- just to test training speed/efficiency of the compression method, not for actual workload prediction

Advantages

1.	Much faster training
2.	Fewer parameter
3.	Better prediction accuracy than older models
4.	Improvement was especially strong for long-term prediction (60 min), showing it captures underlying patterns better, not just short-term trends

Limitations :
1.	  No privacy protection: The model doesn't include any privacy-preserving mechanism, even though cloud workload data can be sensitive 
2.	  Only tested on CPU utilization: Even though the authors claim it could work for memory usage or disk requests, they never actually tested those 
3.	  Small-scale experiment: Only 20 virtual machines and 10 days of data were used — not clear how well it scales to much larger, longer-term cloud deployments 
4.	 Compression vs accuracy trade-off still exists: Higher compression (lower rank R) causes bigger accuracy drop — the "sweet spot" (R=32) was chosen somewhat empirically, not from a strict theoretical rule 
5.	  No comparison against deep reinforcement learning models, which the authors themselves note might perform even better
Research gap
Older cloud workload prediction methods used shallow machine learning (plain neural networks, SVM, linear regression) — these can't capture complex, non-linear patterns well in workload data. No existing work had combined deep learning (for accuracy) with tensor decomposition (for speed) specifically for cloud workload prediction . This paper fills that gap: it keeps the accuracy benefits of deep learning while using CP decomposition to massively cut down training time — solving the "deep learning is too slow to train" problem without sacrificing much accuracy




