Method 
IFSP (Intelligent Fog Service Placement) — a smart system that decides where to place services (containers) on fog nodes (small computers near users, like roadside units) before users even ask for them.
•	The problem it solves: When too many IoT devices (cars, drones, phones) request services, the cloud gets overloaded and slow. Fog computing helps by placing services closer to users — but deciding which service goes on which fog node is extremely hard (NP-hard) because demand keeps changing and resources are limited.
•	Two smart helper components (this is the clever trick of the paper):
1.	IFSS (Scheduler) — decides when and where a change is needed, using an algorithm called R-Learning
2.	Bootstrapper — trains a DRL model offline on the cloud using old server logs before deploying it, so the model isn't "dumb" when it starts
•	Why this matters: Normal reinforcement learning (RL) makes tons of mistakes while learning ("trial and error"). That's dangerous for real users. The bootstrapper pre-trains the model so it's already smart before going live — this avoids slow learning and bad early decisions.
•	Core AI Algorithm — DQN (Deep Q-Network): A reinforcement learning brain that learns which action (place, replace, or do nothing) is best for each container, based on the current situation (state)
•	How the decision-making works (MDP - Markov Decision Process):
•	State: current demand for each service + which container is placed where + which container is being decided right now
•	Action: pick a fog to place the service, remove another service and replace it, or do nothing
•	Cost function (what the AI tries to minimize) : combines 4 goals

Dataset 
1.	Google cluster trace(GCT): real data showing available servers and services that need to be placed.
2.	NASA Server Logs(NSL): 2 months of real website traffic logs that is used to simulate realistic changing user demand patterns over time .


Advantages 
1.	Scales well – tested successfully on both small and large setups 
2.	Adapts to change 
3.	Adapts to changing priorities too- even when weights of 4 objectives changed 
4.	Massively beats heuristic algorithms 
5.	It is Proactive not reactive 

Limitations 
1.	Relies on bootstrapping quality 
2.	No real hardware deployment 
3.	One container at a time 
4.	Complex, multi -part architecture

Research Gap
This paper claims to be the first to solve fog/service placement using Deep Reinforcement Learning, filling the gap by combining an efficient MDP design (one-container-at-a-time actions) with an offline bootstrapper to solve both the exploration-error problem and the slow-convergence problem that plagued earlier DRL attempts
No existing solution combined: proactive prediction + scalable action-space design + fast convergence + real deployment feasibility, specifically for the fog computing service placement problem 











Paper 8
Neural networking based job scheduling cloud 
https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/9169832
S. Tuli, S. Ilager, K. Ramamohanarao and R. Buyya, "Dynamic Scheduling for Stochastic Edge-Cloud Computing Environments Using A3C Learning and Residual Recurrent Neural Networks," in IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing, vol. 21, no. 3, pp. 940-954, 1 March 2022, doi: 10.1109/TMC.2020.3017079.


Method 
A3C-R2N2 — a smart, fast-learning scheduler that decides which edge or cloud computer should run each incoming task, and does it in real time as conditions keep changing.
•	The problem it solves: In an Edge-Cloud setup, some devices (edge nodes) are close to users and fast to respond but weak in power, while cloud servers are powerful but slower to reach. Deciding which task goes where is hard because: task demand keeps changing, network speed changes (devices are mobile), and machines are all different in power/speed/cost.
•	Two clever ingredients combined:
1.	A3C (Asynchronous Advantage Actor-Critic) — instead of one single "brain" learning slowly, this runs many learning agents at once (e.g., on 10+ different edge/cloud nodes), each learning independently and syncing updates periodically. This makes learning much faster and avoids relying on one central point (no single point of failure).
2.	R2N2 (Residual Recurrent Neural Network) — a neural network with "memory" (recurrent layers) that can spot patterns over time (like "this task's CPU need has been rising lately"), plus "skip connections" (residual links) that make training faster and more stable.
•	How the decision works:
•	Input (State): current status of all hosts (CPU/RAM/bandwidth/disk usage, cost, power, response time) + info about tasks that are new or still running
•	Output (Action): for every task, a ranked list of which host it should go to (not just one guess a full priority list, which is safer and easier to train)
•	A separate module (Constraint Satisfaction Module) then checks this ranked list and picks the best valid host (skipping ones that are already full or a task that can't be moved)
•	Loss function (what it tries to minimize): a weighted combination of 5 things energy use, response time, migration time, cost, and SLA violations. The weights can be adjusted depending on what the application cares about most (e.g., healthcare cares about response time, cost-sensitive apps care about running cost)
•	Special design choice: Rather than giving one fixed action, the model outputs probabilities for all hosts  this makes training much easier and avoids the network getting "stuck" trying to output an exact valid combination directly

Dataset
•	Bitbrain Dataset : real-world traces of over 1,000 virtual machines' resource usage (CPU, RAM, network, disk) from real business workloads, recorded every 5 minutes
•	Simulation tool: extended versions of iFogSim and CloudSim (popular cloud/fog simulation toolkits), enhanced to support mobility, bandwidth variation, and edge/cloud cost & power modeling

Advantages 
1.	Scales well-tested with up to 50 learning agents 
2.	Adapts to different priorities by changing weights in the loss functions 
3.	Learns temporal patterns because of recurrent layers 

Limitations 
1.	Fixed max size
2.	GPU memory constraint discovered 
3.	Efficiency drops as agents scale 
4.	Only tested in simulation 
5.	No privacy/security consideration 

Research Gap
No existing work combined: asynchronous multi-agent learning (A3C) + temporal-pattern-aware neural networks (R2N2) + a flexible, tunable multi-objective loss function, specifically for heterogeneous, stochastic Edge-Cloud task scheduling 
This paper fills that gap: it's designed specifically to handle the combination of decentralized multi-node learning, fast adaptability to changing conditions, and awareness of time-based patterns — none of which prior heuristic or DQN/REINFORCE-based schedulers achieved together









