Paper 1
Title: A Deadline-Aware Scheduler for Smart Factory using WiFi 6
Research Gap: The increasing demand for more efficient and precise manufacturing has led to the demand for more smart factories. As the processes become more complex, wired connectivity is getting replaced by wireless technologies such as WiFi, this is not only efficient but also avoids clutter, reduces maintenances costs. An efficient system is needed because failure of processes with non-stringent deadlines might only lead to temporary shutdown but processes with stringent deadlines would lead to shutdown of factory operations or fatal accidents.  A technique typically used for such applications is to first implement such protocols in the digital twin of a factory. Once the digital twin shows acceptable performance, the wireless technology is deployed. This step of integrating the simulation with digital twin leads to a better understanding of bandwidth requirements, the number of packets that miss their latency, and consequences of such misses. Thus, simulation of algorithms used to schedule packets in a specific technology to understand suitability for deployment. WiFi was traditionally not used in factories because of its decentralized nature. This leads to collisions and lack of real-time guarantees. Also, wifi was initially made only for a limited number of users in a small area.
Proposed Solution: The paper proposes a Deadline-Aware Scheduler (LSDS) for WiFi 6-enabled smart factories. It formulates packet scheduling as a Deadline-aware Parallel Machine Scheduling with Synchronized Start (DPMSS) optimization problem, where packets are assigned priority (profit) based on their importance. Since the problem is NP-hard, the authors develop the Local Search Deadline Scheduling (LSDS) algorithm (and LSDSF for fixed Resource Unit configurations), which uses local search and maximum-weight bipartite matching to allocate WiFi 6 Resource Units (RUs) efficiently. The scheduler prioritizes critical packets, maximizes the total profit of packets delivered before deadlines, and improves real-time communication performance in smart factories.
Method: Formulates scheduling as a DPMSS optimization problem and solves it using the LSDS approximation algorithm, which combines local search and maximum-weight bipartite matching for WiFi 6 OFDMA resource allocation.
Advantages:
• Prioritizes deadline-critical industrial traffic.
• Achieves higher packet delivery before deadlines than existing schedulers.
• Reduces critical packet loss.
• Supports real-time industrial communication using WiFi 6 OFDMA.
• Has a theoretical approximation guarantee and practical runtime suitable for industrial deployment.
Limitation:
•	Validated only through simulations, not real factory deployment.
•	Assumes known packet arrivals and deadlines.
•	 Focuses only on WiFi scheduling rather than cloud/container resource management.
•	Does not use AI or reinforcement learning for adaptive scheduling.
•	Future work includes handling stochastic packet arrivals and deadlines.
Dataset: No public dataset is used. The proposed scheduler is evaluated through simulation on five representative smart factory/IIoT scenarios with varying traffic loads. Performance is compared against existing scheduling methods using metrics such as profit ratio, packet drop rate, critical packet drop rate, and execution time.
Paper Link: https://dl.acm.org/doi/epdf/10.1145/3641512.3686387

