Paper 3
Title: A Kubernetes custom scheduler based on reinforcement learning for compute intensive pods
Research Gap: Existing Kubernetes scheduling approaches are primarily designed for general-purpose, network-aware, or edge-cloud environments and do not effectively optimize the placement of compute-intensive containerized workloads. The default Kubernetes scheduler often results in suboptimal resource utilization under dynamic workloads, while many existing AI-based scheduling methods focus on latency or network performance rather than CPU efficiency and energy consumption. Consequently, there is a need for an intelligent scheduling mechanism that can autonomously optimize container placement, improve CPU utilization, reduce energy consumption, and adapt to varying workload characteristics.
Proposed Solution: The paper proposes two reinforcement learning-based Kubernetes schedulers, SDQN and SDQN-n, built on the Deep Q-Network (DQN) framework. These schedulers autonomously determine optimal pod placement for compute-intensive workloads by learning from cluster states and resource utilization. SDQN minimizes average CPU utilization across cluster nodes, while SDQN-n further consolidates pods onto fewer nodes to reduce energy consumption and enable idle-node shutdown. Compared with the default Kubernetes scheduler and LSTM- and Transformer-based schedulers, the proposed models achieve better resource utilization, lower CPU usage, and improved energy efficiency for cloud-native container orchestration.
Method: Proposed two custom Kubernetes schedulers: SDQN (Deep Q-Network based Reinforcement Learning) and SDQN-n (DQN with pod consolidation strategy). Compared them with the default Kubernetes scheduler, LSTM-based scheduler, and Transformer-based scheduler. Performance was evaluated using average CPU utilization per node after scheduling compute-intensive pods.
Advantages: 
• Reduced average CPU utilization by ~10% (SDQN) and >20% (SDQN-n) compared to the default scheduler.
• Outperformed LSTM and Transformer schedulers.
• Better resource utilization for compute-intensive workloads.
• Supports energy-efficient ("green") data centers by enabling node consolidation and shutdown of idle nodes.
• Reinforcement learning policy can be tuned for different workload scenarios.
Limitation: 
• Evaluated only for compute-intensive workloads; general-purpose workloads were not tested.
• Experiments were conducted on a small 4-node cluster, limiting scalability evaluation.
• Focused mainly on CPU utilization; metrics like latency, throughput, and memory performance were not extensively analyzed.
• SDQN-n's pod consolidation strategy may not be suitable for all workload types or high-availability scenarios.
• Requires training and tuning of reinforcement learning models before deployment.
Dataset: No public dataset was used. The authors created a Kubernetes cluster with 4 worker (slave) nodes and generated 50 compute-intensive pods (CPU-bound dummy workloads) to simulate real-world machine learning and scientific computing tasks. Multiple scheduling trials were conducted for comparison.
Paper Link: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3785520.3785532

Paper 4
Title: Novel Genetic Algorithm with Dual Chromosome Representation for Resource Allocation in Container-Based Clouds
Research Gap: Existing server consolidation and resource allocation techniques in cloud environments are primarily designed for virtual machine (VM)-based architectures and cannot effectively address the two-level resource allocation problem in container-based clouds, where containers are allocated to virtual machines and virtual machines are allocated to physical machines. Most existing approaches rely on greedy heuristics or predefined VM configurations, which often result in local optimum solutions, inefficient resource utilization, increased migration overhead, and higher energy consumption. Therefore, there is a need for an intelligent optimization approach that can simultaneously optimize container and VM allocation while improving energy efficiency.
Proposed Solution: The paper proposes a Genetic Algorithm (GA) with a novel dual-chromosome representation for two-level resource allocation in container-based cloud environments. The dual-chromosome encoding simultaneously optimizes container-to-VM allocation and VM-to-physical-machine allocation using customized crossover and mutation operators. By jointly optimizing both allocation levels, the proposed approach achieves better server consolidation, higher resource utilization, lower energy consumption, and improved performance compared with traditional greedy heuristics and existing GA-based methods.
Method: Dual-Chromosome Genetic Algorithm (GA) for two-level resource allocation in container-based clouds. One chromosome represents container allocation order, while the other represents VM type selection. Uses Next Fit for decoding, Order-1 crossover (container chromosome), Single-point crossover (VM chromosome), Switch mutation, and VM type mutation. The fitness function minimizes the total energy consumption of physical machines.

Advantages:
• Simultaneously optimizes container-to-VM and VM-to-PM allocation.
• Novel dual-chromosome representation enables effective crossover operations.
• Produces significantly lower energy consumption than Best Fit Descending and Single-Chromosome GA.
• Finds complementary VM type combinations for better PM utilization.
• Guarantees feasible allocations using Next Fit decoding without extra constraint handling.
Limitation:
• Computational time is about 7× longer than Best Fit Descending.
• Designed for static batch allocation, not dynamic or real-time scheduling.
• Assumes homogeneous physical machines.
• VM overbooking is not allowed, limiting flexibility.
• Performance may decrease when too many VM types are available because the search space becomes larger.
Dataset: AuverGrid real-world workload trace containing application resource requirements. Four test instances with 100, 200, 500, and 1000 containers were created. Experiments used homogeneous PMs (3300 MHz CPU, 4000 MB memory) and three VM-type configurations (5, 7, and 10 VM types).
Paper Link: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8814542

Paper 5
Title:  Enhancing IIoT infrastructures with Kubernetes: Advanced Edge Cluster Management
Research Gap: Existing Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) infrastructures often rely on complex point-to-point communication between industrial devices and software systems, making deployment, management, scalability, and security increasingly difficult. Furthermore, edge devices require centralized configuration and secure lifecycle management to support dynamic manufacturing environments. Therefore, there is a need for a secure, scalable, and standardized edge infrastructure that simplifies deployment, enables centralized management, and supports reliable industrial communication across distributed manufacturing systems.
Proposed Solution: The paper proposes a Kubernetes-based edge deployment strategy for Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) infrastructures using the Factory+ framework and the AMRC Connectivity Stack (ACS). The solution employs Kubernetes clusters to centrally deploy, configure, monitor, and manage edge agents that collect machine data, standardize it using Factory+ schemas, and transmit it securely via MQTT to a centralized data warehouse. By leveraging isolated Kubernetes edge clusters, the framework improves scalability, security, reliability, and maintainability while simplifying the management of distributed smart manufacturing infrastructures.
Method: Proposed a Kubernetes-based edge cluster management architecture for IIoT using the Factory+ framework and AMRC Connectivity Stack (ACS). The solution uses multiple Kubernetes edge clusters, Edge Agents on Cell Gateways, Flux (GitOps) for automated deployment, Helm charts for application deployment, Kerberos for authentication, SealedSecrets for secure credential sharing, MQTT (Sparkplug) for communication, and JSON Schemas to standardize industrial data. Kubernetes provides centralized management, automatic deployment, monitoring, scaling, and self-healing of edge infrastructure.
Advantages:
• Secure edge deployment using Kerberos authentication and SealedSecrets.
• Multiple Kubernetes clusters improve scalability, fault isolation, and security.
• Centralized management of distributed edge devices.
• Automated deployment and updates using GitOps (Flux).
• Kubernetes provides self-healing and automatic workload scheduling.
• Supports heterogeneous industrial communication protocols (OPC UA, MQTT, Modbus TCP).
• Standardized data through JSON Schema and Unified Namespace (UNS).
• Easy integration of new machines and sensors.
• Remote configuration without physical access to edge devices.
Limitation:

•	No benchmark or public dataset used.
•	No quantitative performance evaluation (latency, throughput, CPU, memory, or scalability metrics).
•	No comparison with existing IIoT/Kubernetes management approaches.
•	Deployment depends on Kubernetes expertise and infrastructure.
•	Validation is limited to the AMRC deployment and theoretical use cases.
•	No detailed evaluation of network overhead, energy consumption, or fault tolerance.
Dataset: No public dataset was used. Validation was performed using a real-world deployment of the AMRC Connectivity Stack (ACS) at the Advanced Manufacturing Research Centre (AMRC). The deployment includes 4 cities, 9 buildings, CNC machines, PLCs, Building Management Systems (BMS), energy monitoring devices, and public data sources communicating through OPC UA, MQTT, and Modbus TCP protocols.
Paper Link: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10710186

Paper 6
Title: CSFRL: A Reinforcement Learning Technology Enabled Computing Power Scheduling Framework Based on Kubernetes
Research Gap: Existing Kubernetes scheduling mechanisms rely on the default scheduler, which performs serial pod allocation and lacks the flexibility to support customized scheduling in complex cloud and edge environments. This leads to increased scheduling delays, resource imbalance, reduced resource utilization, unfair task allocation, and inefficient management of heterogeneous computing resources. Furthermore, current Kubernetes scheduling approaches provide limited support for fine-grained resource analysis and intelligent batch scheduling. Therefore, there is a need for an AI-driven scheduling framework capable of fine-grained resource monitoring, adaptive decision-making, and efficient computing resource allocation.
Proposed Solution: The paper proposes CSFRL (Computing Power Scheduling Framework based on Reinforcement Learning), a Kubernetes-based scheduling framework that uses the Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) reinforcement learning algorithm for fine-grained computing resource scheduling. The framework consists of three modules: a Monitoring Module for collecting cluster resource information using Prometheus, a Computing Module that employs PPO with resource sorting to generate intelligent scheduling decisions, and a Scheduling Module that deploys microservices to appropriate Kubernetes nodes. By enabling batch scheduling, fine-grained resource analysis, and adaptive scheduling strategies, CSFRL improves resource utilization, scheduling efficiency, and cluster performance compared with the default Kubernetes scheduler.

Method: Proposed CSFRL (Computing Power Scheduling Framework based on Reinforcement Learning) for Kubernetes. The framework consists of three modules: Monitoring Module (collects CPU/memory usage using Prometheus), Computing Module (uses PPO-based reinforcement learning with resource sorting), and Scheduling Module (deploys pods using Kubernetes API). It performs fine-grained CPU analysis and schedules microservices in descending order of CPU demand to improve load balancing and convergence.
Advantages: 
•	Supports custom Kubernetes scheduling using reinforcement learning.
•	Enables fine-grained CPU resource scheduling.
•	Uses batch scheduling, reducing delays compared to the default scheduler.
•	PPO with sorting achieves faster convergence and better CPU load balancing.
•	Outperforms Kubernetes Default Scheduler, PPO, A2C, and Simulated Annealing.
•	Adapts scheduling decisions to changing workloads through reinforcement learning.
•	Improves overall computing resource utilization in edge computing environments.
Limitation:
•	Considers only CPU resources; memory, storage, GPU, and network bandwidth are not optimized.
•	Evaluated on a small-scale Kubernetes cluster (1 master, 3 workers).
•	Uses only the Online Boutique application, limiting workload diversity.
•	Performance evaluation focuses mainly on CPU load balancing; metrics like latency, throughput, and energy consumption are not considered.
•	Reinforcement learning requires training time and computational overhead.
•	Large-scale real-world deployment was not validated.
Dataset: No public dataset was used. Experiments were conducted using the Online Boutique Microservices Demo Application. CPU usage of 11 microservices was collected using the Hey HTTP stress testing tool. The Kubernetes cluster consisted of 1 master node and 3 worker nodes (NVIDIA Jetson Nano and NVIDIA Jetson Xavier NX devices).

Paper Link: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10294003

Paper 7
Title: An ML-Enabled Reinforcement Learning Model for Efficient Cloud Load Balancing
Research Gap: Existing cloud load-balancing techniques, such as Round Robin and First Come First Serve, fail to adapt to dynamic workloads, resulting in virtual machine overload, poor resource utilization, increased energy consumption, and higher operational costs. Although recent machine learning, genetic algorithm, and reinforcement learning approaches improve certain aspects of scheduling, they often focus on isolated objectives such as energy efficiency, SLA compliance, or autoscaling. Most existing methods lack integrated workload prediction, distributed reinforcement learning, cross-layer VM-container coordination, and simultaneous optimization of resource utilization, energy consumption, and monetary cost. Therefore, there is a need for an intelligent cloud-native scheduling framework that combines predictive analytics, reinforcement learning, autoscaling, and resource-aware optimization for dynamic cloud environments.
Proposed Solution: The paper proposes a Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL)-based dynamic load-balancing framework for cloud computing. The framework integrates machine learning-based workload prediction, Q-Learning and Deep Q-Network (DQN) for intelligent task scheduling, K-Means clustering for virtual machine classification, and Kubernetes-style autoscaling for adaptive resource provisioning. A reward function considering CPU utilization, energy consumption, and monetary cost enables proactive scheduling decisions. Compared with the traditional Round Robin scheduler, the proposed framework achieves better load distribution, lower energy consumption, reduced operational cost, adaptive scalability, and improved system stability.
Method: Proposed an ML-enabled Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) framework for cloud load balancing. The model combines Linear Regression for workload prediction, K-Means Clustering for VM classification, Q-Learning & Deep Q-Network (DQN) for intelligent task scheduling, and Kubernetes-style autoscaling for dynamic resource allocation. The RL reward function optimizes CPU utilization, energy consumption, and monetary cost simultaneously.
Advantages: 
•	Dynamically balances workloads across VMs.
•	Predicts future CPU workload using ML for proactive scheduling.
•	Reduces energy consumption and operational costs.
•	Improves CPU and memory utilization.
•	Uses MARL for adaptive and distributed decision-making.
•	K-Means reduces RL action space, improving scheduling efficiency.
•	Supports Kubernetes-style autoscaling for better scalability.
•	Outperforms traditional Round Robin scheduling in load balancing, energy efficiency, and resource utilization.
Limitation: 
•	Evaluated only in a simulated cloud environment rather than a real cloud deployment.
•	Uses Linear Regression, which may not accurately capture highly nonlinear workload patterns.
•	Training MARL/DQN introduces computational overhead compared to traditional schedulers.
•	Performance under very large-scale cloud infrastructures is not extensively evaluated.
•	Does not consider network latency, bandwidth, or fault tolerance as optimization objectives.
•	Results are primarily compared with Round Robin, with limited comparison against other advanced load balancing algorithms.

Dataset: No public dataset was used. The framework relies on simulated cloud workload data, including historical CPU utilization, VM resource usage (CPU, memory), energy consumption, cost metrics, and Kubernetes pod workloads generated within the experimental cloud environment.
Paper Link: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11582990


OBJECTIVES:
